# Task Brief — <TASK-NAME>

> Copy to `tasks/<TASK-ID>-<task-name>.md` and adapt. This file is the
> contract between the orchestrator and the executor for ONE task.
> Written in British English. Normative keywords per RFC 2119.
>
> Validation-first principle (05 §3): the acceptance criteria are written
> BEFORE the work starts, as exact commands with expected results. The
> executor's self-reported "it passes" is NEVER the conclusion — the
> orchestrator re-runs every command below.
>
> Stop-and-report principle (04 §2): when a chosen technical route fails,
> the executor STOPS and reports back — it never silently switches to an
> alternative route and keeps going. Same for out-of-scope discoveries.

## Metadata

| Field | Value |
|:--|:--|
| Task ID | `<TASK-ID>` (e.g. `task-042`) |
| Brief version | `1.0` |
| Orchestrator | `<agent-ref>` (resolves in the agent registry) |
| Executor | `<agent-ref>` (resolves in the agent registry) |
| Target repo | `<repo-ref>` (e.g. `astra-aigate`) |
| Branch | `<branch-name>` (feature branch; never push it as the deliverable) |
| Date issued | `<YYYY-MM-DD>` |
| Date due | `<YYYY-MM-DD>` |

## 1. Objective

- One paragraph: WHAT this task delivers and WHY (design intent, not a
  list of edits).

## 2. Scope

- **In scope**: `<areas the executor MAY touch — files, modules, layers>`.
- **Out of scope**: `<areas the executor MUST NOT touch — keep explicit>`.
- The orchestrator's git diff review (5.3) is judged against this scope
  declaration.

## 3. Stopping Conditions (MUST)

The executor is headless — the orchestrator is not watching. These are the
four situations where the executor MUST stop and report back instead of
improvising. The report goes to the orchestrator and includes: where it is
stuck, what was tried, the failure evidence, and the suggested way forward.

- **MUST**: when the chosen technical route fails (missing API, dependency
  conflict, design assumption falsified), STOP and report — do NOT silently
  switch to an alternative route and keep going. Route changes are the
  orchestrator's call.
- **MUST**: when an acceptance command fails, STOP and report with the raw
  output and a first-pass root-cause analysis — do NOT modify the command or
  the expected result to manufacture a pass.
- **MUST**: when an out-of-scope need surfaces (bug, side effect, pre-existing
  debt), record it in the report and STOP — do NOT expand the change beyond
  the scope declaration.
- **MUST**: when required information, permission or resource is missing,
  STOP and ask the orchestrator — do NOT fill the gap by guessing.
- **SHOULD**: when the task will clearly overshoot its time/effort budget,
  report progress and the revised estimate before continuing.

## 3.5 Budget (MUST)

The initial working budget for this task. This is a contract term, not a
death sentence — running long is not a failure; overshooting silently is.

- **Initial budget**: <e.g. 30 min wall-clock of focused work, or N turns>.
  The orchestrator sets this when dispatching.
- **70% report (MUST)**: at ~70% of the budget consumed, the executor MUST
  report current progress, a revised estimate, and a requested extension,
  corroborated by its progress signal (files changed / commands run / tests
  passing). Reporting after expiry is meaningless — the decision point is
  before the deadline.
- **Approval (orchestrator-only)**: extensions are granted explicitly by the
  orchestrator and recorded; large extensions escalate to the operator. The
  executor requests, never self-approves (acceptance/approval authority never
  leaves the orchestrator — ADR 0007 §9.2).
- **Grace on expiry**: when the budget expires with a request pending, the
  executor keeps running in grace and waits for the verdict — it is NOT
  killed (killing discards completed work; abnormal completion goes to
  recovery, ADR 0007 §4).
- **Resource guard (separate, non-negotiable)**: a hard ceiling (e.g. 10×
  the budget) is a quota-layer safety net against runaway tasks; hitting it
  forces wrap-up + recovery, never a silent timeout. Negotiable budget (this
  section) and non-negotiable guard are distinct.

## 4. Acceptance Criteria (MUST)

Each criterion is an exact command and its expected result. The executor
runs these during work; the orchestrator re-runs them after hand-off.

| # | Command | Expected result |
|:--|:--|:--|
| 1 | `<exact command, e.g. npx tsc -p tsconfig.typecheck-core.json>` | `<exact expectation, e.g. zero NEW errors>` |
| 2 | `<exact command, e.g. test runner invocation>` | `<exact expectation, e.g. 16/16 smoke tests pass>` |
| 3 | `<exact command>` | `<exact expectation>` |

- **MUST**: every acceptance criterion carries a verification command —
  a criterion without a command is not an acceptance criterion.
- **MUST**: expected results are objective (exit code, counts, output
  strings), not subjective ("should work").
- **SHOULD**: keep the command set minimal — one command per aspect.

## 5. Hand-off Protocol

### 5.1 Deliverables

- `<deliverable 1 — file paths, commit hashes, service endpoints>`.
- `<deliverable 2>`.
- The decision record (5.2) accompanies the deliverables, never replaces
  the diff.

### 5.2 Decision record (MUST)

The executor appends a decision record to the task report: what changed,
why, which alternatives were considered, and why they were rejected.
The orchestrator reviews it for consistency with this brief's scope.
Any stopping-condition event (section 3) and its resolution MUST also be
recorded here — a silent route change is indistinguishable from an
undocumented decision.

### 5.3 Orchestrator verification (MUST)

1. Re-run every command in section 4 — the executor's self-report is
   informational only.
2. `git diff` review against the scope declaration in section 2.
3. Session record (JSONL) kept in the project directory, retrievable on
   demand (05 §5). MUST NOT contain credential values — secrets enter only
   via environment variables and are never logged.

## 6. Definition of Done

The task is complete ONLY when ALL of the following hold:

- [ ] All acceptance criteria in section 4 pass under the orchestrator's
      re-run, not just the executor's run.
- [ ] `git diff` matches the declared scope; nothing out-of-scope changed.
- [ ] Decision record delivered and consistent with the brief; all
      stopping-condition events documented with their resolutions.
- [ ] No placeholder comments ("XXX removed"), no orphan TODOs, comments
      explain WHY (AGENTS.md code hygiene).
- [ ] Branch state recorded for the deliverable push (development branch,
      dual remote) — the feature branch itself is never the deliverable.
